@@ -0,0 +1,70 @@
+## Implementation Plan: Partial Member Spring Intervals
+
+### Purpose
+Allow each member-spring case to define consecutive spring intervals measured from the member I end, while preserving whole-member behavior for existing rows with no length. Keep calculation, the grouped WinForms editor, the 3D view, and printed input consistent with the original member and its entered rows.
+
+### Scope
+- Current state: `FrameWebforCS/components/input/InputFixMemberComponent.cs:96-159` shows a `部材長 (m)` column but does not bind it. `InputFixMemberService.cs:11-26` has no length field. `FrameWebforCS/calculation/CalculationRequest.cs:383-405` omits interval data and drops rows whose spring coefficients are all zero. `InputRigidZoneService.cs:93-107` only serializes rigid-zone input; physical splitting occurs in `FrameWeb/src/fem/legacy_beam.py:42-213`, which currently applies the summed spring stiffness to every segment. `FramePrintPDF/PDF_Manager/Printing/PrintInput/InputFixMember.cs:11-329` neither reads nor prints interval length.
+- Interval contract: for one member, rows are evaluated in their displayed order from the I end. On a 5 m member, lengths `2, 2, 1` define `[0,2]`, `[2,4]`, `[4,5]` m. A sole blank length covers the full member. When explicit lengths and a blank length coexist, only the group's final row may be blank; it covers the positive remaining length. If explicit lengths total less than the member length with no final blank row, the trailing interval has no spring (confirmed by the user). Rows with an explicit length and zero coefficients represent an un-sprung interval and must survive every projection. Legacy groups with no explicit lengths retain their former whole-member additive spring behavior. The proposed serialized field is nullable `length` in metres; absence remains compatible with old files.
+- Editor contract: rows for the same member form one group in each of the six spring sheets. Entering a member number outside its existing group moves that row to the group's end. `\` inserts a row and row-header Delete removes selected rows, while cell Delete clears only cell values. Move the existing keyboard/row-header handling from `InputLoadComponent` into an opt-in, per-sheet API in `myFpSpread` before starting the spring implementation. Enable it for the load-value and member-spring sheets; leave member input and other sheets disabled. Use the installed `GrapeCity.Spread.WinForms.ja` 15.4 `FpSpread` grouping API, rather than only arranging adjacent rows. A collapsed group displays the member's geometric full length in the length column and `***` in spring-value columns; expanded rows show their entered interval lengths and coefficients.
+- Modified files: first `FrameWebforCS/components/myFpSpread.cs` and `FrameWebforCS/components/input/InputLoadComponent.cs` for shared row operations. Then `FrameWebforCS/components/input/InputFixMemberComponent.cs`, `InputFixMemberService.cs`, `FrameWebforCS/providers/InputDataService.cs`, `FrameWebforCS/calculation/CalculationRequest.cs`, `FrameWebforCS/three/ThreeConstraintsService.cs`, `FrameWeb/src/fem/legacy_beam.py`, `FramePrintPDF/PDF_Manager/Printing/PrintInput/InputFixMember.cs`, and the PDF load-diagram spring geometry in `FramePrintPDF/PDF_Manager/Printing/PrintDiagram/DiagramSpring.cs` and `XSpring.cs` where it displays the same input.
+- Tests: preserve and extend `FrameWebforCS/tests/InputLoadFixedRowsTests.cs` and `UnifiedLoadIntensitySheetTests.cs` for the commonized key handling; extend `CalculationRequestTests.cs`, `ThreeConstraintsServiceTests.cs`, `InputViewportSelectionTests.cs`, `FrameWeb/tests/integration/test_beam_foundation.py`, and `test_result_projection.py`; add focused WinForms grouping/row-operation and PDF input/diagram tests under the existing test projects. Cover observable behavior at each projection boundary, including unsprung gaps and the confirmed trailing remainder. Keep `FrameWebforJS/` and `FrameGConverter/` outside this change.
+- Dependencies and references: `FrameWebforCS/FrameWebforCS.csproj:21` pins Spread 15.4.0. The [Spread grouping limitations](https://docs.mescius.jp/help/spread-winforms-15/spwin-group-interop.html) say grouping installs `GroupDataModel` and requires row changes through its target model; [Group.Text](https://docs.mescius.jp/help/spread-winforms-15/FarPoint.Win.Spread~FarPoint.Win.Spread.Model.Group~Text.html) supports a custom collapsed header. The actual `\`/Delete precedent is `FrameWebforCS/components/input/InputLoadComponent.cs:113-169`; the checked-in `InputMembersComponent.cs` has no row-operation handler. The user confirmed member input remains OFF. Preserve the currently uncommitted direct-PDF and viewport work in `FramePrintPDF/` and `FrameWebforCS/`.
+
+### Implementation Steps
+
+Complete shared row operations and the Spread binding proof first, then preserve one interval contract through persistence, calculation, display, and printing.
+
+#### Step 0: Extract opt-in row operations into myFpSpread
+- [ ] Introduce a per-`SheetView` opt-in API in `myFpSpread` with service-owned insertion/deletion callbacks. The control detects plain `\` outside edit mode and row-header selections followed by plain Delete, gathers selected row indices once, and leaves ordinary cell Delete and protected-cell behavior intact. Default is OFF for every sheet; no shared control directly mutates a service `BindingList`.
+- [ ] Migrate the load-value sheet to this API and remove its duplicate key/mouse plumbing while retaining its case-selection and revision notifications. Keep load-name and member-input sheets OFF. Preserve current fixed 100,000-row service semantics, refused operations, and selection behavior.
+**Verification**: Existing load operation tests plus focused shared-control STA tests cover opt-in/off sheets, modified keys/edit mode, row-header-only disjoint multirow deletion, cell Delete, failed callbacks, callback errors, selection, and disposal. Finish this gate before editing the spring implementation.
+
+#### Step 1: Prove the Spread group/editor contract
+- [ ] Build a focused 15.4.0 WinForms experiment or test using the actual bound spring sheet: group by member number, collapse/expand, show the full geometric length in the length column and `***` in every spring-value column, and map a clicked/edited detail row back to its stable source row. Prove the reverse mapping used by viewport selection and expand the target group before selecting a hidden detail row. This folded presentation is a required pass condition.
+- [ ] Verify `GroupDataModel.TargetModel` behavior when a row is inserted, deleted, moved, or rebound. Avoid grouping all 100,000 vacant rows into an expensive blank group; choose and document a bounded active-row view or another demonstrated approach. Prove how the installed API renders full length and `***` in their respective folded-group columns; a single composite `Group.Text` is insufficient for that presentation.
+- [ ] Record the working API sequence and performance/selection result in the implementation review before relying on it in the editor. If the installed API cannot meet the required folded summary and row operations together, return for a design decision instead of silently replacing grouping with an outline or manual row hiding.
+**Verification**: An automated STA test plus a manual small and large sheet check shows grouping, exact folded text, expanded editability, source-row mapping, and usable interaction with many vacant capacity slots.
+
+#### Step 2: Store intervals and implement grouped row operations
+- [ ] Add nullable `length` to `clsFixMember`, bind both 2D/3D length columns, include it in display snapshots and save/load. Validate finite, positive explicit lengths and original member IDs; preserve missing length on old files.
+- [ ] Register each spring sheet with the Step 0 shared row-operation API and define service-owned atomic insertion, row-header multirow deletion, and automatic move to an existing member group's end. Preserve fixed maximum capacity, reject insertion if the final slot is occupied, renumber shifted `row` values, rebuild the sparse index once, publish one calculation-input revision, and restore selection after Spread finishes editing. Rebuild Spread groups without losing the selected case or expansion state where possible.
+- [ ] During saved-file load, collect the prepared node, member, and spring data in `InputDataService.JsonDataOpen`, then validate each spring group's cumulative lengths against the prepared geometric member length before applying any service state. In `InputFixMemberService`, canonicalize old noncontiguous rows into contiguous member groups in first-appearance order, preserving each member's relative row order and legacy additive whole-member behavior. Renumber the resulting rows and validate the mixed-length rule before commit.
+- [ ] Validate groups after moving, inserting, or deleting. For mixed lengths reject a nonterminal blank, zero/negative remainder when a terminal blank is present, and cumulative explicit length beyond the geometric length. For a group with explicit sum shorter than the member and no terminal blank, leave the trailing span un-sprung as requested. Keep old all-blank groups as full-member springs.
+**Verification**: Focused service and STA editor tests cover `2+2+1` on 5 m, automatic grouping after a distant edit, insertion/deletion and selection, blank terminal remainder, explicit zero-stiffness gap, malformed file rollback with all services unchanged, capacity boundary, old noncontiguous-file normalization and whole-member result, and one revision per atomic operation.
+
+#### Step 3: Carry the exact row contract into calculation requests
+- [ ] Extend `CalculationRequestBuilder.NormalizeConstraintRows` for `fix_member` to retain ordered `length` values and explicit zero-coefficient interval rows; keep `fix_node` normalization unchanged. Reject invalid group shape and lengths before calling Python, using the same interval rules as the editor.
+- [ ] Preserve original member IDs, case selectors, and input rows in the calculation JSON. Keep physical segmentation out of the C# JSON projector: Python's existing member preparation already creates segments before FEM assembly and maintains load, rigid, joint, notice-point and result mappings.
+**Verification**: `CalculationRequestTests` assert exact projected row order, length, zero-gap retention, selector behavior, whole-member fallback, and errors for invalid intervals; compare saved JSON with the request to ensure the original input is not rewritten into generated members.
+
+#### Step 4: Split spring intervals in the Python model preparation
+- [ ] In `legacy_beam.prepare_members`, add cumulative spring boundaries to the same coalesced positions used by rigid zones, notice points, and loads. For each generated segment, sum only the selected spring rows covering its physical span and assign zero foundation elsewhere. Snap near-equal boundaries consistently and reject zero-length or out-of-range intervals.
+- [ ] Before `legacy_beam.select_case` reduces `fix_member` to the selected sheet, retain the spring rows referenced across load cases, analogous to `_all_member_loads`. Build the union of their interval boundaries in every case's mesh while applying only that case's selected spring sheet. This preserves the canonical topology checked by `analysis_result_sets.py:50-61` when cases choose different spring sets. Keep original member IDs, end releases, load locations, rigid material assignments, and existing load-transfer-member foundation rejection.
+**Verification**: Python integration tests check exact per-segment foundation for `2+2+1`, an unsprung trailing remainder, an explicit zero-stiffness gap, old full-length rows, overlap with rigid/load/notice boundaries, endpoint tolerance, and cross-case identical topology with differing stiffness. Compare a partial-foundation response with an independent segmented beam reference and retain existing full-length results.
+
+#### Step 5: Show and print the same intervals
+- [ ] Change `ThreeConstraintsService.cs:117-130` so its spring-row loop advances an I-end cursor in saved/displayed `row` order for every explicit row, including zero-stiffness gaps, and passes each active interval's center and length to `AddFixMember` instead of the original member's center and length. Sort `InputFixMemberService.GetDisplaySnapshot` by `row`, because its current sparse list uses edit order after a row is changed. Interpret terminal blank and legacy all-blank groups exactly as the request builder and Python preparation do. Preserve local-axis orientation and case-specific spring values.
+- [ ] Bound the repeated glyph anchors and visible geometry to the active interval, including short intervals where the current repetition spacing (`ThreeConstraintsService.cs:538-575`) can overshoot and the axial spring line can degenerate to one vertex. Check the axial range of the line vertices as well as the anchor positions, including after scale changes. Keep the per-row pick proxy/selection position within that interval and map its stable source row back to the correct expanded Spread detail row after grouping or automatic row movement. Preserve the existing 250,000-vertex total budget and the old scene on budget failure. `ThreeService.cs` already rebuilds constraints on spring edits, case switches, topology changes, and file replacement; modify it only if the grouped row mapping requires a new call path, and preserve its concurrent edits.
+- [ ] Extend PDF `Printing/PrintInput/InputFixMember.cs` to parse and print the entered interval length in 2D and 3D, including Japanese/English/Chinese headings and units. Adjust 2D paired-table widths and pagination. Print original input rows, not generated solver segment IDs. Preserve blank legacy length semantics in the output.
+- [ ] Update conditional PDF load-diagram spring drawing in `DiagramSpring.cs`/`XSpring.cs` to advance through every interval, omit zero-stiffness rows, and clip glyphs to active spans so a diagram does not claim a full-member spring after the calculation changes. Keep existing direct-PDF work intact.
+**Verification**: 3D view tests locate glyph anchors and geometry inside `[0,2]` and `[2,4]` on a 5 m member, leave `[4,5]` empty, distinguish a zero-stiffness interior gap from the terminal remainder, and verify the correct source row on pick after grouping and an out-of-order edit. Recheck short spans, sloped members and `cg`, local axes, case switches, edit/reload refresh, scale changes, the total vertex budget, and preservation of the old scene after budget failure. PDF tests inspect original input-row lengths in 2D and 3D, blank-length rendering, multilingual headings, 2D paired-table pagination, and diagram geometry for an explicit zero-stiffness gap and a terminal remainder. Build the touched .NET projects.
+
+#### Step 6: Verify end-to-end behavior and preserve concurrent work
+- [ ] Run focused C# and Python tests, then the relevant .NET build and Python integration gate. Review calculation output, saved input, reopened editor grouping, 3D view, and generated PDF from the same multi-interval example.
+- [ ] Use a behavior matrix when reviewing test coverage: (1) whole-member legacy blank, (2) exact partition `2+2+1`, (3) short explicit sum with no blank and unsprung tail, (4) explicit zero-spring interior gap, (5) final blank remainder, (6) invalid lengths and nonterminal blanks, (7) grouping/move/insert/delete with source-row selection, (8) cross-case topology and selector changes, and (9) coincidence with rigid/load/notice boundaries. For each relevant layer, assert its observable result: editor/persistence, C# request, Python mesh/response, 3D geometry and pick, PDF input and diagram. Record deliberately inapplicable cells rather than inferring coverage from a test name. No repository-wide numeric coverage threshold is established for this feature.
+- [ ] If line coverage is collected, use the available `dotnet-coverage` collector for touched C# code as a diagnostic and report it alongside the behavior matrix. The locked Python development environment currently lacks `coverage`/`pytest-cov`; add a compatible locked dependency only if numerical Python coverage is needed. Do not substitute a percentage for the interval-specific assertions above.
+- [ ] Inspect the final diff against the pre-existing dirty paths and avoid overwriting the concurrent direct-print/viewport changes. Run `git diff --check`; run `.agents/check.ps1` because this plan is under `.agents/`.
+**Verification**: The 5 m / `2+2+1` case gives the same intervals in save, request, mesh, view, and PDF; old input with no lengths gives its former result; all relevant gates pass or environment/concurrent failures are reported separately.
+
+### Risks & Considerations
+- Spread grouping uses `GroupDataModel`; the [official limitations](https://docs.mescius.jp/help/spread-winforms-15/spwin-group-interop.html) explicitly restrict row-count changes after grouping and require edits through `TargetModel`. The current six fixed 100,000-row lists and large vacant tail make a proof with actual package 15.4.0 essential before committing to a binding design.
+- Grouped display indices differ from source `row` indices. Insertion, deletion, auto-move, selection, save order, and 3D highlighting must use stable source identities and only publish a completed atomic change.
+- The desktop 3D view currently draws each spring row over the full original member. Partial intervals require span-aware anchors and pick positions; very short spans must be checked against the repeated-glyph spacing and scaling so the drawing does not extend into an unsprung gap.
+- An all-zero explicit interval is structural input because it advances the I-end cursor. The current C# projector drops it; losing that row silently shifts every later spring interval.
+- Different spring selectors can produce different split points. A union of boundaries is required for multi-case result topology, but selected stiffness must remain case-specific.
+- Literal splitting in C# before the Python call would duplicate the existing Python geometry transformation and require remapping loads, joints, rigid zones, notice points, and result IDs. This plan splits before FEM assembly in Python's legacy preparation phase; the solver receives split elements.
+- PDF input tables and PDF load diagrams are distinct output paths. `FramePrintPDF/PDF_Manager/PrintInput.cs`, `PdfDocument.cs`, `PDF_Manager.csproj`, and new direct-print files, plus current viewport work, already have unrelated uncommitted edits and must be preserved.
+
+### Open Questions
+- The installed Spread 15.4 API must be proven to render the required per-column folded summary while supporting editable grouped rows. Step 1 treats this as a pass/fail feasibility gate; the plan will be revised if the API cannot do both.
